  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nAn upper low tied to the subtropical jet stream will slowly \nmeander across northern Mexico and Texas over the weekend and \ninto early next week, yielding expansive, persistent ridging \nacross the central CONUS. The polar jet stream will remain \nactive with a series of disturbances getting shuttled eastward \nand southeastward across southern Canada and the northern and\neastern Great Lakes during this time frame. However, the core \nof this jet will remain displaced far enough to our north to \nkeep these disturbances and any associated precipitation a safe \ndistance away from our forecast area. Thus, the net result of \nthis weather pattern for northern Illinois and northwestern \nIndiana will be precipitation-free conditions and a warming \ntrend from near-normal high temperatures today to above normal \nhigh temperatures (likely in the low-mid 60s) by Monday and \nTuesday. Low temperatures will also generally trend upward from \nthis morning's widespread freezing/near-freezing conditions, \nthough additional near to slightly below freezing lows could be \nobserved in some spots again Sunday morning and once more Monday\nmorning.\n\nClear nocturnal skies and light/calm winds may lead to some \nlocalized patchy fog development this morning in a few \nlocations, but this isn't expected to become a widespread issue.\nInterestingly, multiple pieces of forecast guidance are \nsignaling that advection fog may drift into our area \ntonight/Sunday morning as modest low-level warm/moist advection \nin advance of a surface trough/weak cold front attempts to \nsaturate the planetary boundary layer. An examination of HRRR, \nRAP, and RRFS forecast soundings suggests that there may be just\nenough separation between surface temperatures and the steadily\nrising surface dew points to have this influx of low-level \nmoisture manifest itself as more of a low stratus deck, but at \nleast localized visibility reductions at ground level do appear \nto be a distinct possibility tonight into Sunday morning -- \nparticularly with northward and westward extent across northern \nIllinois.\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in relatively good agreement on a \npattern change occurring during the latter half of this upcoming\nweek as several upper-level disturbances amalgamate to yield \ndeep aggregate longwave troughing across the northeastern \nquadrant of the CONUS. This troughing will likely end up \nencompassing most or all of the Great Lakes region, and there \nhas been an increasing ensemble signal for lake effect \nprecipitation occurring in our forecast area late next week as a\nresult. Individual shortwaves embedded within the longer \nwavelength troughing could also deliver a broader wave or two of\nsynoptic precipitation to the area during the mid-late week \ntime frame as well, though there has been less spatiotemporal \nconsistency and overall agreement amongst Grand Ensemble members\non this possibility being realized. As the composite longwave \ntrough becomes established, a cooler air mass will also \nbarge into the region and could end up influencing what types \nof precipitation are seen here late next week. Just how cold \ntemperatures will get in our area late next week into next \nweekend will depend on the exact longitudinal positioning of the\nlongwave trough axis, which is an item of relatively low \nconfidence at this time.\n\nOgorek",
